  "help.preview": "Preview controls\n- Drag: rotate · wheel: zoom · hold Space and drag (a hand) or right-drag: pan\n- Direction buttons refit the view from the front/back/left/right/top/iso\n- View: source colours / palette (after merge) / print simulation (predicted colours of physical + virtual mixes). 'Side by side' shows palette and simulation together\n- Flat colours: colour values without lighting or shading (for comparison)\n- Flat colours and soft edges: the toolbar buttons switch both views; in the side-by-side view the small buttons beside each label switch that view only\n- Background: the checkerboard keeps background and model colours apart; the slider sets its brightness (preview and colour map)\nSelecting patches\n- Hovering shows, in light iris, the patch a click would take\n- Click: select one patch (connected faces of the same colour), outlined in white\n- Double-click: outline every patch of that colour (colour mode). Now Shift+click adds a patch or removes a selected one; a plain click takes that patch alone and ends colour mode\n- Shift+click or Ctrl+click (⌘+click): add/remove patches. A plain click always takes one patch alone\n- Click empty space or Esc: clear everything. 'Exit colour mode' keeps the selection and only hides the colour-mode outlines\n- The selection panel names the extruder those patches print with (VE virtual mix with its recipe, or E physical)\n- 'Fill selection' (or Enter) gives every selected patch the colour chosen under 'Change to'. Undo (Ctrl+Z) and Redo (Ctrl+Shift+Z) are also toolbar buttons\nOutline colours\n- Light iris: the colour under the pointer (merge table, spectrum) or the patch a click would take · white with a dark rim: selected patches, colours checked in the merge table, the colour-mode colour\nColour map\n- The faces unwrapped into 2D. It shows the same selection as the 3D view; click, double-click and Shift+click there select just like in 3D\n- Drag (Space+drag while brushing): pan · wheel: zoom · ⤢: fit\n- Picking a patch in 3D moves the map to it; picking on the map centres the 3D view on it (the view you clicked in stays put)\n- Charts are cut where the surface turns more than 70° from the chart's facing direction, so a shape with a front and a back becomes at least two pieces\nBrush\n- Turn on 'Brush' to paint with a left-drag in both the 3D view and the colour map (rotate 3D with the right button, pan with Space+drag)\n- Turned on with patches selected, 'Only inside the selected patches' switches on, so nothing outside the selection is painted. Turn it off to paint anywhere\n- Pick the colour in the brush bar; the radius is in model units (mm), also set with the slider at the bottom left of the preview or the [ ] keys. The brush circle of one view is mirrored in the other\n- Each drag is one undo step: Ctrl+Z or Undo reverts it\n- Eyedropper (or Alt+click): takes the colour of the face you click, in the model or the map, as the brush and 'Change to' colour. Handy for filling a small patch with the colour of the big one next to it\nFill (G)\n- Click a patch to fill all of it with the colour chosen in the bar above. Hovering shows what would be filled, already in that colour\n- Clicking inside the selection fills the whole selection\n- Each click is one undo step (Ctrl+Z)\nGap clean (C)\n- Between picks (default): click patches of two colours (each click adds), then Enter or [Clean]. Bits of another colour wedged a face or two deep between them go to the colour they border longer\n- Picked bits: the other way round, pick the wedged bits themselves (double click = every place of that colour). Each melts from the outside in, into the colour it borders most where it sits; a bit between two colours is shared between them. Places 1 mm across or more on the print (set on the bar) are shown one by one first, with a question whether to keep them\n- Whole model: the same between any two colours, everywhere. A warning comes first: protect colours that must stay (eye and mouth lines) in 02\n- Protected colours never change, and each run is one undo step (Ctrl+Z)",
  "help.load": "Load\n- Drop a Rodin 3D Print 3MF exported with Output Mode = Face Color, or a vertex-colour OBJ\n- The model and colour work are autosaved and restored next time (on the public site inside this browser; GYUROMA on your own computer keeps them in its user-data folder). 'Start over' clears the model and the saved work (settings stay)\n- Save work file: keeps the model, colours, cuts, colour bands and hand-set recipes as one file (.gyuroma). Even when the browser's store is cleared, drop it in the box above — in any browser, on any computer — and it opens as it was\n- Original colours: puts the loaded file's own colours back (⌘Z takes that back)\n- Palette colours: reduces the model to this many colours (median cut); independent of the merge table\n- Click a colour in the palette table to change its value\n- A PrusaSlicer template 3MF provides the bed size and the per-extruder filaments (colour and name), which fill the physical slots E1–E5 automatically; keep the checkbox on and it is attached automatically next time",
  "help.filaments": "Physical filaments\n- Physical extruders = tool heads of the printer (XL 5T = 5)\n- Click a slot's colour box for the picker (HEX / RGB), or type #RRGGBB in the field next to it\n- Filament list: one per line as 'name; type; #colour'. Prefix a line with E1: to put it straight into that slot. Use the Slot column or 'From list…' to assign, ▲▼ to reorder\n- 'Fill from model palette' copies palette colours; 'Suggest from list' picks the filaments that fit the palette best\n- Accurate colour values give accurate mix predictions",
  "help.merge": "Colour merge\n- Select checkboxes: merge several colours into one at once\n- Click a colour to change its value; hover outlines it in blue in the preview, checking it in yellow\n- Merge into: the colour this one is merged into\n- Shade/boundary: when on, faces of this colour are left empty during the merge and re-judged by the majority of the connected surface (keeps shadows from sticking to the wrong colour)\n- Protect: excluded from cleanup (jaggy smoothing, island absorption). Use for small accent colours\n- Min. island: islands smaller than this many faces are absorbed by the surrounding colour. Recommended: skin 60, dark colours 120, others 150\n- Run merge → the report compares area before / plain merge / after re-judgement. Undo reverts",
  "help.mix": "Colour mix settings\n- Assignment mode: physical only / physical + virtual mixes. Virtual mixes are PrusaSlicer ColorMix layer-alternating blends\n- Max colours per mixture: physical filaments in one virtual colour (2 recommended). Resolution 'thirds' = ratios in steps of 1/3\n- Mapping strategy, colour difference (ΔE00 recommended) and accent preservation decide which blend each palette colour gets\n- Preview brightness: display only, no effect on the exported file (0 = none)\n- Auto physical-direct: palette colours within this ΔE00 of a physical filament go straight to that filament instead of a mix\n- The table below lets you force a physical filament per palette colour\n- Physical + virtual up to 255 (PrusaSlicer 2.9.6+). More colours mean longer slicing and printing",
